.\" Manual page for gmsh2nmsh
.TH GMSH2NMSH 1 "@MAN_DATE@" "Neko @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gmsh2nmsh \- convert a Gmsh mesh to Neko's nmsh format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gmsh2nmsh
.I msh-file
.RI [ nmsh-file ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gmsh2nmsh
reads a mesh in Gmsh's
.B .msh
format and writes the equivalent Neko native mesh in
.B .nmsh
format, without going through Nek5000's
.B .re2
format. It is part of the Neko contrib tools and is installed only when
Neko has been configured with
.BR --enable-contrib .
It does not use MPI or the Neko library, see
.B NOTES
for building it on its own.
.PP
The input can be in MSH format 4.1 or 2.2, ASCII or binary. It must consist
of first or second order hexahedra (3D) or quadrilaterals in a plane of
constant z (pseudo-2D). The 8, 20 and 27 node hexahedra and the 4, 8 and 9
node quadrilaterals are accepted. A quadrilateral mesh is written as a 2D
.B .nmsh
file, which Neko extrudes into a periodic slab one element thick when it
reads the mesh, as it does for 2D meshes from
.BR rea2nbin .
Tetrahedra, prisms, pyramids, triangles and
elements of order 3 or higher are rejected.
.PP
The conversion does the following:
.IP \(bu 2
Boundary facets in physical groups (surfaces in 3D, curves in 2D) become
labeled zones. When all their tags are valid zone indices (1 to 20), the zone
index is the physical tag. Otherwise the groups are numbered 1, 2, ... in
increasing tag order. The mapping is printed as a table.
.IP \(bu 2
Pairs of physical groups given with
.B \-\-periodic
become periodic zones. The two groups of a pair must be related by a
translation and their facets must match one to one.
.IP \(bu 2
For second order elements, edges whose midpoint is off the straight line
between the edge end points are stored as midpoint curves, which Neko uses to
build a quadratic geometry. Face and volume centre nodes are not used.
.IP \(bu 2
Left-handed elements are mirrored so that all elements are right-handed.
.PP
When physical groups are defined, Gmsh only saves the elements that belong
to one. The volume (3D) or surface (2D) must therefore be in a physical
group, or the mesh must be saved with
.BR "Mesh.SaveAll = 1" .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I msh-file
Input mesh file. Must end in
.BR .msh .
.TP
.I nmsh-file
Optional output file name. Must end in
.BR .nmsh .
When omitted, the output name is derived from
.I msh-file
by replacing the
.B .msh
suffix with
.BR .nmsh .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-periodic= pairs
Make pairs of physical groups periodic. Groups are given by tag or by name,
the two groups of a pair are separated by a colon and pairs by a comma, for
example
.B 1:2
or
.BR inlet:outlet,front:back .
The option can be repeated. Names containing spaces or separator
characters must be given by tag.
.TP
.BI \-\-tol= value
Absolute tolerance used to match periodic facets and vertices. The default
is 1e-6 times the shortest facet edge of the pair.
.TP
.BI \-\-curve\-tol= value
Distance between an edge midpoint and the straight edge, relative to the
edge length, above which the edge is stored as curved. The default is 1e-4.
.TP
.B \-\-linear
Ignore the high-order nodes and write straight-sided elements.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The mesh was converted successfully.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred. Typical causes include an unsupported format version or
element type, a mesh without hexahedra or quadrilaterals, or periodic
groups that do not match under a translation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a second order mesh with Gmsh and convert it:
.PP
.RS
.B gmsh pipe.geo -3 -order 2
.br
.B gmsh2nmsh pipe.msh
.RE
.PP
Convert a channel mesh that is periodic in x and z:
.PP
.RS
.B gmsh2nmsh channel.msh \-\-periodic=xmin:xmax,zmin:zmax
.RE
.PP
Check the result:
.PP
.RS
.B mesh_checker pipe.nmsh
.RE
.SH NOTES
Within Neko's build, the tool has its own configure script and is compiled
with the plain Fortran compiler
.B FC
rather than
.BR MPIFC ,
so it links neither MPI nor any of Neko's dependencies.
.PP
The tool is a single Fortran 2008 source file without dependencies, so it can
also be compiled on its own, for example on the machine where the mesh is
generated:
.PP
.RS
.B gfortran -O2 gmsh2nmsh.F90 -o gmsh2nmsh
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR neko (1),
.BR rea2nbin (1),
.BR prepart (1)
.PP
Project documentation and user guide:
.UR https://neko.cfd
.UE
